course: dictionary
lesson: 11621
title: Approximate: close, but not exact
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Approximate means close to the right answer, but not exactly right. You use it when the exact number is hard to find, not needed, or not known yet. «The bus journey takes approximately twenty minutes.» The bus might take 18 or 22 minutes, so twenty is a good guess, not a promise.
explain: As a verb, to approximate means to make a close guess. «Let's approximate the total before we pay.» You can round £4.90 to about £5 and add up quickly. The answer is close to the real total, but it is not the exact total.
explain: In maths you will see the sign ≈, which means «is approximately equal to». «π ≈ 3.14» tells you that pi is close to 3.14, but its digits go on forever, so 3.14 is not exact. If your answer is close to a rough estimate, it is probably right.
fact: Approximate means close to the exact amount, but not exactly the same.
fact: «Approximate» is an adjective: an approximate answer, an approximate value.
fact: «Approximately» is the adverb: «The rope is approximately ten metres long.»
fact: The sign ≈ means «is approximately equal to»: 3.14 ≈ π.
fact: Rounding gives an approximate answer: 296 rounds to about 300.
exercise: «The approximate value of the car was £8,000.» What does approximate mean here? | The exact value to the penny | The value is zero | *Close to the exact value, but not exact | A value that is very high
exercise: Round 47 to the nearest ten. What is the approximate answer? | 4 | *50 | 40 | 47
exercise: Which sign means «is approximately equal to»? | < | *≈ | = | >
test: Approximate 198 + 603 by rounding each number to the nearest hundred. What is the approximate answer? | 700 | 900 | *800 | 801
test: Which word is the adverb of approximate? | approximate | *approximately | approximation | approximates
exam: Ana pays £4.98 for a book and says it cost about £5. What kind of number is £5? | An exact price | A price far too high | *An approximate price, close to £4.98 | A price with no value
